<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsugaru Nobuhira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1586">1586</a>–<a href="/years/1631">1631</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-clan">Tsugaru clan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsugaru Nobuhira was the second lord of the Hirosaki domain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-tamenobu">Tsugaru Tamenobu</a>. He completed Hirosaki Castle in 1611 and laid out the castle town around it. Under him the domain, carved out of former Nanbu lands, was secured under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsugaru-nobuhira.jpg" alt="Tsugaru Nobuhira" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsugaru_Nobuhira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tsugaru Nobuhira was born on 9 May 1586, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-tamenobu">Ōura Tamenobu</a>, head of the Ōura clan. In 1596 he and his two elder brothers, Nobutake and Nobukata, are known to have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 he accompanied his father in the Eastern Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and served in Ieyasu's retinue, while Nobutake was a page to Toyotomi Hideyori at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. As with the Sanada clan, the division ensured that the family would survive whichever side won.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When his father died, Nobuhira became head of the clan over the objections of a faction that supported his nephew Tsugaru Kumachiyo, the young son of Nobutake. The dispute, the first of many internal conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-clan">Tsugaru clan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, put the domain at risk of attainder, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> arbitrated and confirmed Nobuhira as daimyō. To secure his standing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> he married Mate-hime, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and the widow of Fukushima Masayuki; his first wife, Tatsu-hime, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>, was reduced to the status of concubine and sent to the clan's small holding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozuke-province">Kōzuke Province</a>.</p><p>He built the castle at Takaoka in haste, demolishing other castles of the domain for their materials, and its five-storey donjon was far larger than usual for a lord of 47,000 koku. In 1614 he sent troops to support the Tokugawa in the Osaka Winter Campaign. In 1619 the clan was ordered to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a> to make way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a>, but with the help of the priest Nankōbō Tenkai the order was turned aside. In September 1627 lightning set the donjon on fire and a store of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> exploded; the castle was rebuilt on a smaller scale and renamed Hirosaki Castle in August 1628.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nobuhira developed the port of Aomori on Mutsu Bay for shipping to Edo and for passage to the northern island of Ezo, and he raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> production with new paddy fields and irrigation and by bringing in craftsmen from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He died at the clan residence in Edo in 1631 and was buried at Shinryō-in, a subsidiary temple of Kan'ei-ji in Tokyo. His eldest son, Tsugaru Nobuyoshi, born to Tatsu-hime, succeeded him, while Tsugaru Nobufusa, his son by Mate-hime, received a holding of 5000 koku at Kuroishi and was the ancestor of the later lords of Kuroishi Domain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsugaru Nobuhira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Nobuhira</b> completed Hirosaki castle in 1611.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirosaki-domain">Hirosaki Domain</a></li></ul></section>
